<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Finistère</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Finistère is the westernmost <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> of mainland <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, at the tip of the Breton peninsula, with its prefecture at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a> and the naval port of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a>. Its name comes from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/latin">Latin</a> finis terrae, 'end of the land'. Created in 1790 from part of the old province of Brittany, it covers most of the ancient territory of the Osismii.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/finistere.jpg" alt="Finistère" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Higlander029 · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Prefecture_de_Kemper_(_finistere).JPG" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Finistère lies in the extreme west of Brittany. Its prefecture is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a>, and its largest city is <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brest-france">Brest</a>. Other large towns include Concarneau, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morlaix">Morlaix</a>, Carhaix, Quimperlé and Douarnenez, and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> includes the island of Ushant, called Eusa in Breton and Ouessant in French. It is the westernmost department of metropolitan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and has a claim to being the most coastal: 117 of its 277 communes lie on the coast, and its coastline of about 1,250 km makes up almost a quarter of that of Brittany.</p><p>The abers, rugged fjord-like inlets, are a notable feature of the north coast. The Pointe de Corsen, at the north-western tip of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>, is the westernmost point of continental <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>. About 40 kilometres to the south lies the rugged and isolated headland of the Pointe du Raz.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The present <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a> consists of the historical region of Léon and parts of Cornouaille and Trégor, all of them parts of Brittany before the Revolution. Its name comes from the Latin Finis Terræ, meaning the end of the earth. The Breton name, Penn ar Bed, means head or end of the world and resembles the Cornish name of Land's End, Pedn an Wlas. It should not be confused with Fisterra in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/galicia">Galicia</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/spain">Spain</a>, which has the same origin.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>Agriculture, fisheries and food processing occupy an important place in the economy. A military presence, including the Île Longue submarine base and the naval air base at Lanvéoc-Poulmic, together with related industries, employs a significant part of the population. The port of Roscoff has ferry links with Ireland and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-great-britain">Great Britain</a>.</p><p>Finistère is the area where Breton survives most strongly as a spoken language, and its Breton-speaking schools are called <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diwan">Diwan</a>, Divyezh and Dihun. The Festival de Cornouaille in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/quimper">Quimper</a> celebrates Breton music and traditions. The sculptor Roland Doré made many of the calvaries that stand in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/departement">department</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Finistère” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The Osismii lived in <b>Finistère</b> and western Côtes-d'Armor.</blockquote><a href="/state/osismii">Osismii</a></li></ul></section>
